tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{SFT vs.\ regex: semantic understanding.}
The nomarker ablation (\S\ref{sec:nomarker}) provides the primary evidence: with all format markers removed, SFT achieves 97.65\%$\pm$0.96 (Qwen-7B, 3 seeds) and 97.82\% (Qwen-1.5B), a 4.04pp gap over the best regex baseline (B4, 93.61\%).
On the 8 boundary samples where pass-labeled code triggers exceptions, SFT correctly classifies 6/8 while the regex misclassifies all 8 (McNemar $p{=}0.041$), confirming that SFT learns to distinguish caught from uncaught exceptions.

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Calibration and Defect Prediction.}
LLM calibration \citep{kadavath2022language,spiess2025calibration} measures whether model confidence aligns with actual correctness; our finding that \texttt{exception\_only} achieves ECE ${<}$ 0.007 across three architectures suggests that compact, high-specificity inputs improve calibration.
Just-in-time defect prediction \citep{kamei2013large} shares our motivation of early fault detection, though it operates on code change features rather than execution traces.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Calibration and Defect Prediction.}
LLM calibration research \citep{kadavath2022language,spiess2025calibration} studies confidence-correctness alignment; our ECE ${<}$ 0.007 finding suggests compact inputs improve calibration.
Just-in-time defect prediction \citep{kamei2013large} shares our early fault detection motivation but operates on code change features rather than execution traces.

\paragraph{Why exception\_only outperforms ae\_only.}
Although \texttt{ae\_only} is a strict superset of the exception signal, multi-seed evaluation reveals that the extra content introduces noise: \texttt{ae\_only} achieves 99.64\%$\pm$0.49 while \texttt{exception\_only} achieves 99.83\%$\pm$0.00.
The zero-variance result indicates that \texttt{exception\_only} captures a minimal sufficient signal, while \texttt{ae\_only}'s additional components (fallback locals, assertion formatting) introduce seed-dependent variance.
